<header><p class="enc-kicker">Religion / idea · religion</p><h1 class="enc-title">Khalsa</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Khalsa is the collective body of initiated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sikhism">Sikhs</a>, established by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guru-gobind-singh">Guru Gobind Singh</a> in 1699 as a community bound by shared discipline, identity and readiness to defend the faith. It provided the ideological and military core from which the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sikh-empire">Sikh Empire</a> emerged under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ranjit-singh">Ranjit Singh</a>. Khalsa identity shaped the empire's army, its claims to legitimacy and, after Ranjit Singh's death, the factional politics of its decline.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/khalsa.jpg" alt="Khalsa" width="320" height="396" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Shubhdeep Sandhu · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Khanda_(Sikh_Symbol).svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The word khalsa comes from an Arabic word meaning pure or sincere. Under Mughal rule it first meant land held directly by the emperor, not granted to lords as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jagir">jagir</a>. Before <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guru-gobind-singh">Guru Gobind Singh</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sikhism">Sikh</a> community was organised through agents called masands, who collected revenue. Khalsa came to mean loyalty directly to the Guru and not to these intermediaries, who were increasingly seen as corrupt. The term appears in letters of earlier Gurus, such as Guru Hargobind, who referred to a congregation as the Khalsa of the Guru.</p><p>According to the popular account, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guru-gobind-singh">Guru Gobind Singh</a> gathered the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sikhism">Sikhs</a> at Anandpur Sahib on 13 April 1699, the day of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vaisakhi">Vaisakhi</a>. He drew his sword and asked for a volunteer willing to give his head, and five men came forward in turn.</p><p>Each was taken into a tent, and the Guru returned with a bloodied sword; in the end all five emerged unharmed, and the blood was that of goats. He called them the Panj Pyare, the first Khalsa, and initiated them with amrit, water and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sugar-plantation">sugar</a> stirred with a double-edged sword. He then asked them to initiate him, and his name changed from Gobind Rai to Gobind Singh.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>Initiation is called Amrit Sanchar or Khande di Pahul, and anyone of any previous religion, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caste">caste</a> or age may take it. A male initiate takes the name Singh, meaning lion, and Kaur became the compulsory name for women in the twentieth century. The Khalsa wear the Five Ks: uncut hair, a wooden comb, an iron or <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/steel">steel</a> bracelet, a sword or dagger, and short breeches. Its rules forbid tobacco and intoxicants, adultery, and meat slaughtered in the Muslim manner, and a member who breaks them must be initiated again. The collective body of initiated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sikhism">Sikhs</a> is known as the Guru <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/panth">Panth</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>The creation of the Khalsa abolished existing social divisions, and conservative followers protested; many left the ceremony. A Mughal newswriter, Ghulam Mohyiuddin, reported to the emperor that the Guru had abolished <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caste">caste</a> and custom and bonded his followers into one brotherhood. In October 1708 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/banda-singh-bahadur">Banda Singh Bahadur</a> was sent to lead the Khalsa against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mughal-empire">Mughals</a>.</p><p>Later the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dal-khalsa">Dal Khalsa</a>, a coalition army, fought the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mughal-empire">Mughals</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afghans">Afghans</a>, which led to the misls and then to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sikh-empire">Sikh Empire</a>. By the death of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ranjit-singh">Ranjit Singh</a> in 1839 its army was assessed at 120,000 men with 250 artillery pieces. Today <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sikhism">Sikhs</a> respect the Khalsa although not all are initiated, and it displays martial skills at the festival of Hola Mohalla.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Khalsa” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>He recognised that the traditional <b>Khalsa</b> cavalry, however effective in irregular warfare, could not match disciplined modern forces.</blockquote><a href="/empire/sikh-empire">Sikh Empire · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Sikhs believe that the light of the one Guru passed through all ten human Gurus, and that since 1708 the Guru has been present in the scripture, the Guru Granth Sahib, and in the community of initiated Sikhs, the <b>Khalsa</b> Panth.</blockquote><a href="/religion/sikhism">Sikhism · Beliefs &amp; Practice</a></li><li><blockquote>It is chanted in the morning and evening prayers of the gurdwaras, at the initiation ceremony of the <b>Khalsa</b> and at cremations.</blockquote><a href="/songs/japji-sahib">Japji Sahib</a></li></ul></section>
